WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.930
The animated hit Zootopia won a boatload of
awards and critical acclaim, and for good

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:10.190
reason — the movie was pretty much fantastic
from start to finish.

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:13.680
But that doesn't mean it was an easy path
to success:

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.440
"Fine, then we’ll have to do this the hard
way."

00:00:17.369 --> 00:00:22.760
From creative shake-ups late in the game,
to a few… interesting moves during the marketing

00:00:22.769 --> 00:00:27.419
campaign, here's the untold truth of Disney's
Zootopia.

00:00:27.419 --> 00:00:29.739
Darker early draft

00:00:29.739 --> 00:00:34.090
The world of Zootopia wasn't always quite
so cheery.

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:38.150
It was reported that an early draft of the
script presented the city as "broken," with

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:43.030
a veneer of utopia but a "dark dystopian truth
underneath."

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:44.030
The problem?

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:48.920
Well, this version of Zootopia didn't really
feel like a place a person would actually

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:52.920
want to visit, and the studio worried viewers
wouldn't connect to the city if it wasn't

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.149
a place they fell in love with.

00:00:55.149 --> 00:01:00.629
They backed off that approach, which led to
the vibrant and gorgeous Zootopia we ended

00:01:00.629 --> 00:01:01.629
up getting.

00:01:01.629 --> 00:01:04.570
​Kristen Bell’s slow cameo

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:09.530
Actress Kristen Bell is a bit on the quirky
side, and apparently one of her greatest obsessions

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:11.570
is sloths.

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:15.820
This obsession became very public when her
husband, Dax Shepard, surprised her with the

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:19.310
news that a sloth would be at her birthday
party.

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:23.580
And she was so excited that she had a complete
emotional meltdown.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:28.240
"Are you so sad that one's coming to your
party?"

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.140
"I'm so excited."

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:33.930
The video of the actresses freak out quickly
became a viral sensation.

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:38.250
It was such a big deal that word got back
to the Zootopia team, and they reached out

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:42.799
to Bell for a cameo as the voice of the lady
sloth, Priscilla.

00:01:42.799 --> 00:01:44.930
Shifting protagonists

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:50.320
The story at the heart of Zootopia is fairly
unique for a Disney flick, and it turns out

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.800
that it actually changed a lot during the
latter part of production.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:58.380
Instead of telling the story from the perspective
of do-gooder Judy Hopps, the original story

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:03.000
largely took place from the viewpoint of the
wily fox Nick Wilde.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.830
Director Byron Howard explained that the story
took a "huge shift" midstream, because it

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:10.190
was hard to actually like Nick.

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:14.709
Howard also made it clear that he wanted audiences
to start to fall for Nick at the same time

00:02:14.709 --> 00:02:20.780
as Judy does, and that proved difficult when
he was presented as the hero from the get-go.

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.170
Global newscasters

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:27.590
For the most part, Zootopia was pretty much
the same film no matter which country or market

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:28.830
you watched it in.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:33.690
But there was one subtle change that most
people didn’t even know about: the animal

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:35.000
newscaster.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:40.349
Disney tailored the character to animals that
were popular and a good fit for each country.

00:02:40.349 --> 00:02:42.860
In the U.S. and Canada, we saw a moose.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:44.680
In Japan it was a tanuki.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:49.750
Australia got a koala, and in China it was
a panda.This is cool thing to do, but most

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:54.959
people would never even have noticed it unless
they went on a worldwide tour of Zootopia

00:02:54.959 --> 00:02:56.000
viewings.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:57.950
Shakira’s curvy demands

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:04.730
This is a Disney movie, after all, so of course
it had to feature a catchy musical number.

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:09.819
That fell to real-life singer Shakira, who
voiced the animal pop star Gazelle and gave

00:03:09.819 --> 00:03:12.159
us the hit song "Try Everything."

00:03:12.159 --> 00:03:16.930
But Shakira also had some pull when it came
to her character's look.

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:22.760
The early drawings of Gazelle were apparently
very skinny, so Shakira asked the designers

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:25.709
to give her character some curves.

00:03:25.709 --> 00:03:30.040
Disney obliged, and we ended up getting a
Gazelle whose hips were incapable of telling

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:31.120
falsehoods.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:32.769
​Nick's middle name

00:03:32.769 --> 00:03:39.739
In Zootopia, the sly but loveable fox Nick's
full name is actually Nicholas P. Wilde.

00:03:39.739 --> 00:03:42.189
But what the heck does the "P" stand for?

00:03:42.189 --> 00:03:47.761
Director Byron Howard revealed Nick's middle
name is officially "Piberius," which is a

00:03:47.761 --> 00:03:51.319
subtle nod to Captain James Kirk's middle
name, Tiberius.

00:03:51.319 --> 00:03:54.790
But how alike are these two characters, really?

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:59.120
"It’s called a hustle sweetheart, and I’m
not the liar - he is."

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:03.320
"Hey!"

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:07.450
"Now look Krako, we're taking over the whole,
uh, ball of wax.

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:11.319
Now, you cooperate with us and maybe we'll
cut you in for a piece of the action."

00:04:11.319 --> 00:04:16.190
Now that you mention it, Nick does have a
bit of that same roguish charm.

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:17.200
Car talk

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:22.100
The team behind this film took their world-building
responsibilities seriously, to the point of

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:27.730
bringing in automotive design legend J Mays
to help develop the concepts for the different

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:31.610
designs that all these various animal species
might drive.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:33.780
What would a giraffe's car look like?

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:35.330
Or a hippo's SUV?

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:39.090
Those were the types of questions the filmmakers
put to Mays.

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:45.130
The designer took his job seriously and made
sure that each of the vehicles seen in the

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:48.800
film properly represented the animals they
were carrying.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:50.820
A sense of scale

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:57.190
The design team used accurate scales to excellent
effect for some amazing action set pieces

00:04:57.190 --> 00:05:03.130
in Zootopia, but even more went into those
scenes than most may have realized.

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:07.640
Every single animal represented in Zootopia
is made to scale, meaning the rabbits match

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:12.580
up just like they should when standing beside
a lion, and a giraffe really is that much

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:14.420
bigger than a fox.

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:19.050
That design work even makes it into the world
itself, with different areas and buildings

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:22.260
designed for specific types of animals.

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:23.680
Disney Easter Eggs

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:30.130
A Disney movie about a world of walking, talking
animals is a veritable playground for Easter

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:33.680
eggs, and this one took full advantage.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:37.410
It would take a while to name them all, but
here are a few.

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:43.040
The calendar hanging on the wall in Chief
Bogo's office features a shot of San Fransokyo

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:44.810
from Big Hero 6.

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:49.320
Another scene features two elephant kids ice
skating in Frozen outfits.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:55.710
And several Disney flicks get the spoof treatment
on the weasel’s bootleg DVD cases.

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:56.710
The backlash

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:01.680
At this point it’s not really a secret to
anyone that while the movie is a lot of fun,

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:07.740
Zootopia also tells a very poignant story
about racial messaging, nature over nurture,

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:09.600
and the way we judge others.

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:13.960
"It may have something to do with… biology."

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:19.940
But that high-minded approach to a kid's movie
didn't sit well with everyone.

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:24.330
Zootopia was met with a bit of a backlash
once the film became a major hit, with some

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:29.930
pundits calling it a "muddled mess" and accusing
the film of taking itself too seriously.

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:34.590
We aren’t sure how founded these complaints
really were as Zootopia definitely had no

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:38.190
trouble receiving acclaim come awards season.

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:40.350
A furry frenzy

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:45.170
Though Disney films are typically marketed
to families, the studio took a very sneaky

00:06:45.170 --> 00:06:49.880
route to promote Zootopia to one very specific
subculture: furries.

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:55.661
If you don't know, furries are folks who like
to dress up as animals and… well… get

00:06:55.661 --> 00:06:57.470
together to have a good time.

00:06:57.470 --> 00:07:02.720
Apparently, Disney contracted a marketing
firm to promote the film to furry fan groups,

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:07.600
with a plan to encourage them to tweet and
share their own photos using film-specific

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:08.780
hashtags.

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:11.480
"Yes, Twitter exploded.

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:13.240
Twitter s--- itself."

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:18.150
Disney never actually acknowledged the play,
but the paper trail definitely seemed to prove

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:19.290
it was true.

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:21.520
Regardless, the film was a huge hit.

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:23.700
So, umm, maybe it worked.

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:27.740
Funnily enough, this isn't the first time
talking animals have had a clash with the

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:29.030
law on-screen:

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:31.210
"My friends call me 'Sexy.'"

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:33.060
"Oh, lucky you."

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:34.200
Thanks for watching!

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:37.150
